<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	App-level privacy manifest. Without this, App Store Connect rejects the UPLOAD with ITMS-91053
	(missing required-reason API description) before a human reviewer ever opens the build.

	EVERY ENTRY BELOW IS TRACED TO A REAL CALL. A wrong reason code fails the upload as ITMS-91055,
	which is the same outcome as no file at all, so nothing here is declared "to be safe".

	AUDITED 14 August 2026 across the app's own Swift and all twelve Capacitor packages. Full evidence
	in docs/privacy-manifest-report.md. Summary of what was NOT found, because a category declared
	without a matching call is its own defect:
	  - FileTimestamp    NOT DECLARED. No creationDate / modificationDate / contentModificationDateKey /
	                     attributesOfItem anywhere. The Capacitor core uses FileManager.fileExists,
	                     removeItem and createDirectory, none of which is a required-reason API.
	  - DiskSpace        NOT DECLARED. No volumeAvailableCapacity, systemFreeSize or statfs. The one
	                     nearby call is WebViewAssetHandler.swift's .fileSizeKey, which is a single
	                     file's size, not free disk space, and is not in the category.
	  - SystemBootTime   NOT DECLARED. No systemUptime, mach_absolute_time, kern.boottime or sysctl.
	  - ActiveKeyboards  NOT DECLARED. No activeInputModes.

	If a plugin is added or upgraded, RE-RUN THE AUDIT. Only @capacitor/ios ships its own manifest
	(two, both declaring nothing); the other eleven packages ship none, so anything they touch has to
	be declared HERE.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
				UserDefaults — required because @capacitor/preferences is the backing store for the
				offline order outbox (lib/native/outbox.ts, keys hg_outbox_*).
				TRACED TO:
				  node_modules/@capacitor/preferences/ios/Sources/PreferencesPlugin/Preferences.swift:19
				      return UserDefaults.standard
				  …/PreferencesPlugin.swift:89,93,112,114  (dictionaryRepresentation, string(forKey:),
				      removeObject(forKey:))
				That package ships NO PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy of its own, so this declaration must live here.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<!--
					CA92.1 — "access information from user defaults to read and write information that
					is only accessible to the app itself."
					CHOSEN OVER 1C8F.1 ON EVIDENCE, NOT BY DEFAULT. 1C8F.1 is for defaults shared with
					other apps via an App Group, and this project has neither:
					  - no com.apple.security.application-groups key in App.entitlements or
					    AppRelease.entitlements;
					  - exactly ONE PBXNativeTarget in project.pbxproj, productType
					    com.apple.product-type.application — no .appex, no extension, no embed phase;
					  - the plugin calls UserDefaults.standard and never UserDefaults(suiteName:).
				-->
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
		NSPrivacyTracking — FALSE, from evidence rather than assumption. Apple defines tracking as
		linking user or device data with third-party data for targeted advertising or advertising
		measurement, or sharing it with a data broker. The native binary imports only UIKit, WebKit,
		Network and Capacitor; there is no analytics, attribution or advertising SDK anywhere under
		ios/. PostHog is a WEB dependency (package.json) loaded inside the WebView, not linked into
		the binary. No IDFA, no ATT prompt, no ad network.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
		DELIBERATELY EMPTY, PENDING DOMINIC'S DECISION.
		This declaration must AGREE with the App Store Connect privacy questionnaire, it carries legal
		weight, and it is not a developer's call to make. The audit found exactly one candidate — the
		APNs device token, obtained by @capacitor/push-notifications and stored in
		van_devices.push_token — and it sits on a genuinely arguable line: the TOKEN is obtained by
		native code, but it is TRANSMITTED by JavaScript (lib/native/device.ts saveDeviceConfig →
		fetch('/api/native/bind-device')) which ships in the remotely-loaded web bundle, not in this
		binary. See docs/privacy-manifest-report.md Part B for both readings.
		An EMPTY array is a positive statement that the binary collects nothing, so this must be
		revisited before submission — it is not a placeholder that can be left by default.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
